.Dd November 14, 2026
.Dt CPIO 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm cpio
.Nd copy files into and out of archives
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl o
.Op Fl 0aBcLvV
.Op Fl C Ar size
.Op Fl D Ar directory
.Op Fl F Ar archive | Fl O Ar archive
.Op Fl H Ar format
.Op Fl R Ar owner
.Op Fl -quiet
.Li < Ar name-list
.Nm
.Fl i
.Op Fl bBcdfmnrsStuvV
.Op Fl C Ar size
.Op Fl D Ar directory
.Op Fl E Ar file
.Op Fl F Ar archive | Fl I Ar archive
.Op Fl H Ar format
.Op Fl R Ar owner
.Op Fl -absolute-paths
.Op Fl -insecure
.Op Fl -no-overwrite
.Op Fl -no-preserve-owner
.Op Fl -quiet
.Op Ar pattern ...
.Nm
.Fl p
.Op Fl 0adlLmuvV
.Op Fl D Ar directory
.Op Fl R Ar owner
.Op Fl -no-preserve-owner
.Op Fl -quiet
.Ar directory
.Li < Ar name-list
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility copies files into an archive
.Pq copy-out, Fl o ,
out of one
.Pq copy-in, Fl i ,
or from one directory tree to another
.Pq pass-through, Fl p .
Exactly one of the three is given;
.Fl t
alone means
.Fl it .
.Pp
Copy-out and pass-through read the names of the files to take from
standard input, one per line (one per NUL-terminated string with
.Fl 0 ) ,
as
.Xr find 1
writes them.
A directory named is taken by itself, not with its contents.
A name too long for a path is reported and passed over.
.Pp
Copy-in takes every member of the archive, or with
.Ar pattern
operands only those whose names match one of them as
.Xr fnmatch 3
matches with no flags: a
.Ql *
matches a
.Ql /
too.
.Ss Formats
.Bl -tag -width "hpodc, hpbin"
.It Cm newc
The SVR4 portable format: ASCII headers in hexadecimal, magic 070701.
The default for copy-out.
.It Cm crc
newc with magic 070702 and a checksum of each regular file's data,
which copy-in checks.
.It Cm odc
The old portable format: ASCII headers in octal, magic 070707.
This is the cpio interchange format of
.Xr pax 1 .
.It Cm bin
The old binary format, in the writing machine's byte order; copy-in
reads either order.
.It Cm hpodc , hpbin
odc and bin with device numbers as HP-UX writes them.
They cannot be told from odc and bin, so copy-in reads them only when
named with
.Fl H .
.It Cm ustar
The POSIX tar format.
.It Cm tar
The old tar format, which has no devices or fifos.
.El
.Pp
Copy-in finds the format of its archive, other than hpodc and hpbin,
by itself.
A file too large for the format is left out with a diagnostic: newc,
crc and bin hold 4\~GiB less one byte, odc, ustar and tar 8\~GiB less
one.
.Xr cpio 5
describes the formats.
.Ss Links
Copy-out writes a file with more than one link as each format expects.
newc and crc give every name but the last a size of zero and the last
the data, so the names are held back until all have been listed (or
the list ends); odc and bin put the data with every name; ustar and tar
write the later names as links to the first.
.Pp
Copy-in restores the links, the data coming with any of the names;
names whose data never comes become an empty file and links to it.
Pass-through copies the names of one file as links to one copy, or
with
.Fl l
links each file to its source.
.Ss Modes, times and owners
Copy-in and pass-through give each file its archived (or source)
permissions exactly, set-user-ID, set-group-ID and sticky bits
included: the umask does not apply.
A directory is made writable by its owner at first and given its own
mode, and with
.Fl m
its time, once everything has been extracted, so that a read-only
directory still takes its contents.
.Pp
When run by root, copy-in and pass-through also give each file its
archived (or source) owner, by number, unless
.Fl -no-preserve-owner
is given.
.Pp
Without
.Fl u ,
a file on disk no older than the one that would replace it is kept,
with the diagnostic
.Dq not created: newer or same age version exists .
.Ss Safe extraction
By default copy-in refuses a member whose name is absolute or has a
.Sq ..
component, and writes nothing through a symbolic link that the archive
itself makes and that could lead out of the tree (one whose target is
absolute or has a
.Sq ..
component): such links are made only once the archive has been read,
an empty file holding each name until then.
Symbolic links already on disk are followed as usual.
.Fl -absolute-paths
allows absolute names, which are then extracted at that path, and
.Fl -insecure
turns all of this off.
.Pp
On copy-out
.Nm
writes the archive in blocks, 512 bytes unless
.Fl B ,
.Fl C
or
.Fl -block-size
says otherwise, the last padded with zeros.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Ss Modes
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl o , Fl -create
Copy-out: write an archive of the files named on standard input.
.It Fl i , Fl -extract
Copy-in: extract an archive's members.
.It Fl t , Fl -list
List the members, one name per line on standard output, rather than
extracting them; the safety checks do not apply.
.It Fl p , Fl -pass-through
Copy the files named on standard input under
.Ar directory .
.El
.Ss Archive and format
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl F Ar archive , Fl -file Ns = Ns Ar archive
Read or write
.Ar archive
rather than standard input or output.
.It Fl I Ar archive
The archive copy-in reads.
.It Fl O Ar archive
The archive copy-out writes.
.It Fl H Ar format , Fl -format Ns = Ns Ar format
The archive format: one of those above.
.It Fl c
The same as
.Fl H Cm newc .
.It Fl B
Blocks of 5120 bytes.
.It Fl C Ar size , Fl -io-size Ns = Ns Ar size
Blocks of
.Ar size
bytes.
.It Fl -block-size Ns = Ns Ar n
Blocks of
.Ar n
\(mu 512 bytes.
.El
.Ss Copy-out and pass-through
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl 0 , Fl -null
Names on standard input end with a NUL byte rather than a newline.
.It Fl a , Fl -reset-access-time
Put back each file's access time after reading it.
.It Fl L , Fl -dereference
Take what each symbolic link points to rather than the link.
.El
.Ss Copy-in
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl f , Fl -nonmatching
Take the members that match none of the patterns.
.It Fl E Ar file , Fl -pattern-file Ns = Ns Ar file
Read more patterns from
.Ar file ,
one per line.
.It Fl n , Fl -numeric-uid-gid
With
.Fl tv ,
show owners and groups as numbers.
.It Fl r , Fl -rename
Ask on the terminal what to call each member; an empty reply skips it.
.It Fl s , Fl -swap-bytes
Swap the bytes of each 16-bit halfword of each regular file's data.
.It Fl S , Fl -swap-halfwords
Swap the halfwords of each 32-bit word.
.It Fl b , Fl -swap
Both.
A file that is not a whole number of halfwords (or words) is extracted
unswapped in that respect, with a warning.
.It Fl -absolute-paths , Fl -absolute-filenames
Extract members with absolute names, at those paths.
.It Fl -no-absolute-paths , Fl -no-absolute-filenames
Refuse members with absolute names; the default.
.It Fl -safe-extract
Refuse members with
.Sq ..
components and hold back the archive's escaping symbolic links; the
default.
.It Fl -insecure
Turn off all of the safety checks.
.It Fl -no-overwrite
Keep anything already at a member's name.
.El
.Ss Pass-through
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl l , Fl -link
Link each file to its source where they are on one file system, and
copy it otherwise.
.El
.Ss Copy-in and pass-through
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl d , Fl -make-directories
Make leading directories as needed.
.It Fl m , Fl -preserve-modification-time
Give each file its archived (or source) modification time.
.It Fl u , Fl -unconditional
Replace files whatever their age.
.It Fl -no-preserve-owner
When run by root, leave the files made owned by root.
.It Fl -numeric-owner
Give owners by number, which is all an archive records; the default as
root.
.El
.Ss Any mode
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl D Ar directory , Fl -directory Ns = Ns Ar directory
Change to
.Ar directory
first.
.It Fl R Ar owner , Fl -owner Ns = Ns Ar owner
On copy-out, record this owner in the archive; on copy-in and
pass-through, give it to every file made.
.Ar owner
is
.Ar user : Ns Ar group ,
.Ar user . Ns Ar group ,
.Ar user
(the group left as it is),
.Ar user :
(the user's login group) or
.Pf : Ar group ,
each a name or a number.
.It Fl v , Fl -verbose
Name each file as it is archived, extracted or copied, on standard
error.
With
.Fl t ,
list each member as
.Ql ls -l
does: mode, links, owner, group, size (or major and minor numbers),
modification time and name, and a symbolic link's target.
.It Fl V , Fl -dot
Write a dot to standard error for each file, and a newline at the end.
.It Fl -quiet
Do not report the number of blocks.
.El
.Pp
The options come before the operands;
.Ql --
ends them.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
Every file was archived, extracted or copied.
.It 1
Some were not, each with a diagnostic; the rest were.
.It 2
A fatal error: a bad command line, an archive that could not be opened
or written, or one that ends before its trailer.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Archive a tree, and list the archive:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ find src -print | cpio -o > src.cpio
$ cpio -itv < src.cpio
.Ed
.Pp
Extract it elsewhere, making directories and keeping times:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ mkdir out && cd out && cpio -idm < ../src.cpio
.Ed
.Pp
Extract only the C files, from a compressed archive:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ gzip -dc src.cpio.gz | cpio -id '*.c'
.Ed
.Pp
Copy a tree, names with newlines and all:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ find src -print0 | cpio -p0dm /backup
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
Each diagnostic is written to standard error and begins
.Dq Li cpio:\& ,
then names the file or member concerned.
.Pp
At the end of a run that did not fail,
.Nm
reports how many blocks it wrote, read or copied, as
.Dq Ar n Li blocks ,
unless
.Fl -quiet
is given.
.Pp
On copy-in, bytes that are not a header where one is due are skipped
to the next header, with a warning of how many; a malformed header is
reported and passed over.
An archive that ends before its trailer is a fatal error:
.Dq premature end of archive
if no header was found,
.Dq premature end of file
otherwise.
.Pp
On copy-out, a file that yields fewer bytes than it had when it was
examined is padded with zeros to that size, with a warning, so that the
archive stays readable.
A failure to write the archive is fatal.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr find 1 ,
.Xr pax 1 ,
.Xr tar 1 ,
.Xr cpio 5
.Sh STANDARDS
.Nm
was specified by
.St -p1003.1-88
and left out of POSIX from IEEE Std 1003.1-2001 on; its odc format
remains the cpio interchange format of
.Xr pax 1 .
This
.Nm
follows GNU cpio where a standard is silent: its options, formats,
diagnostics and block count.
The safety checks of copy-in are its own.
